{"text": "M. ALLISON MOORE\n(By)\n\nPROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE\nRepublican National Committee Washington, DC\nNational Press Secretary February 2015-present\ne Primary press contact and on-the-record spokesperson for all national media inquiries.\ne Daily collaborations with senior staff in all departments to identify opportunities and successfully sell positive news articles about the\nRNC to national print and broadcast reporters.\ne Manage state and regional press secretaries, as well as communicate internal positions to members of the RNC and other surrogates\nto ensure consistent messaging across all mediums nationwide.\ne Responsible for briefing and staffing the Republican National Committee Chairman for national TV, radio and print interviews, press\nconferences, debates, and other high-profile media events.\ne Played an integral role in developing and marketing budgeted media campaigns, which helped garner over a billion dollars in earned\nmedia for the RNC over the 2016 election cycle.\ne Worked closely with networks, vendors, and press to manage the planning, production, and credentialing for media filing centers at\nGOP Presidential Primary Debates.\n\nMcConnell Senate Committee, Re-Election Campaign \u201814 Louisville, KY\nPress Secretary August 2013 - December 2014\ne Led the press operation for U.S. Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell\u2019s re-election campaign, which resulted in a 25 point victory\nin the primary election and a 16 point victory in the general election.\nPrimary on-the-record spokesperson for all campaign communications.\nSuccessfully pitched hundreds of stories with local and national journalists in order to shape and drive the campaign's narratives.\nTraveled with Leader McConnell to campaign media events.\nOversaw rapid response efforts during the debate and other high-profile media events throughout the campaign.\nManaged press assistants and delegated responsibilities as necessary to ensure maximum attendance and positive coverage of all\nmedia events.\n\nUnited States Senate, Senate Republican Communications Center Washington, DC\nBroadcast Communications Advisor March 2010 - August 2013\ne New position created by the Leader's office following consistently excellent performance reviews and senior staff feedback.\ne Coordinated and managed all of Senate Republican Leader Mitch McConnell\u2019s national media appearances, including CBS\u2019 60\nMinutes and a special \u201cInside Congress\u201d edition of Dateline NBC.\ne Primary radio booking contact for all Republican senators.\ne Developed lasting relationships with several hundred TV and radio hosts and producers nationwide in order to orchestrate media\nappearances for all Republican senators.\ne Assisted in crafting and executing Republican media strategy by providing hosts/producers with daily updates and specific messaging\nangles crafted by the SRCC.\ne One of few staff chosen by Leader McConnell to travel to Kentucky to assist in the final weeks of Sen. Rand Paul's election campaign\nin 2010.\n\nDeputy Press Secretary January 2009 - March 2010\ne Organized and executed two successful virtual radio rows within one week for all Republican senators, earning recognition in the\nradio industry\u2019s Talkers Magazine as a source for producers and hosts nationwide.\ne Developed and maintained effective relationships while serving as the primary point of contact between the SRCC and all radio\n\nmedia.\ne Provided radio hosts and producers across the country with daily updates, audio clips, and other information to assist in their\nbroadcasts.\nUnited States Senate, Office of the Senate Republican Leader Washington, DC\nStaff Assistant June 2007 \u2014 January 2009\n\ne  Greeted and attended to various groups, diplomats, and heads of state prior to meetings and events with the Leader.\ne Communicated the Leader\u2019s position to hundreds of constituents calling his leadership office on a daily basis.\n\nEDUCATION\nUniversity of Kentucky Lexington, KY\nBachelor of Arts, Political Science, May 2007",
